though their contributions are often overlooked. They could be involved in every stage of a 
medical device’s lifecycle (22). This includes: 
•	 assessment of needs and R&D;
•	 innovation in medical devices;
•	 manufacture;
•	 coordinating and supporting clinical validation and evaluation;
•	 regulatory approval and clearance of medical devices;
•	 HTA, pricing, procurement and disposal of devices;
•	 HTM of devices, maintaining, ensuring quality and promoting safe use of medical devices in hospitals 
and other health-care settings;
•	 post-market surveillance and monitoring; and
•	 training.
BMEs could be responsible for implementation of national medical devices policies. At regional and local 
levels, BMEs manage the medical devices in their institution, always in collaboration with other members of 
the health workforce. 
Despite their critical role, BMEs are frequently excluded from decision-making nationally and in health 
facilities, although global recognition of their value is

